# MindWave Prompt Standard — Pre-Execution Gate (MW-PS v1.0)

**Anchor:** AI does not fail intention. It only executes instruction. Governance is the difference.

## Pre-Execution Requirement

Before executing any user request (code change, tool run, refactor, or multi-step plan):

1. **Check MW-PS compliance** of the request or your interpretation of it.
2. If the request is underspecified or intention exceeds instruction, **clarify or restate** with the user using the four layers below before proceeding.
3. Do **not** assume intent, underspecify instructions, leave intelligence undefined, or omit termination.

## Mandatory Four Layers (Every Request Must Map)

| Layer | Purpose | Required |
|-------|---------|----------|
| **I — Intent** | WHY: objective, success conditions, non-negotiables, preservation constraints | Declare what must be true when done |
| **II — Instructions** | WHAT/HOW: allowed/forbidden actions, ambiguity handling, escalation | Every intention must map to ≥1 explicit instruction |
| **III — Intelligence boundary** | HOW TO THINK: decision priorities, error handling, risk posture, when to ask | Define reasoning/behavior; **missing = MW-PS violated** |
| **IV — Termination & continuity** | WHEN TO STOP: completion criteria, post-completion state, context persistence | Prevent default reversion and unintended continuation |

## Compliance Checklist (Hard Constraints)

A request fails MW-PS if any of these are true; **do not execute** until resolved (clarify with user or restate):

- Intentions outnumber governing constraints
- Intelligence is implied but not defined
- Termination conditions are absent
- Ambiguity handling is unspecified

## Minimal Template (Use When Restating or Clarifying)

```
[INTENT] Purpose, success criteria, non-negotiables.
[INSTRUCTIONS] Explicit actions, constraints, ambiguity handling.
[INTELLIGENCE DEFINITION] What good reasoning and behavior mean here.
[TERMINATION & CONTINUITY] Completion, stop conditions, next state.
```

## Canonical Invariants

- Intent is never assumed. Instructions are never underspecified.
- For every intention expected, there must exist an explicit instruction or constraint that binds it.
- Unbound intention will not be inferred; it will be ignored after the instruction completes.
- Default behavior is governed, not leaked.
